speechtext:
Then I will say it at some other time, Mr. Speaker. If all parliament has to do is to spend its time discussing academic questions like this, the sooner we go home the better. A private member has no rights here; yet a private member is the only connecting link between the government of the day and the electors, and when he ceases to function all parliamentary government is at an end. I have, as an authority

for the functions and rights of private members and their duty, that statement in Edmund Burke, who in his address to the electors of Bristol said:
Certainly, gentlemen, it ought to be the happiness and glory of a representative to live in the strictest union, the closest correspondence, and the most unreserved communication with his constituents-
